      <h2 class="section-title">1. What is a Hash Value?</h2>
      <p>A <strong>hash value</strong> (also called a hash, message digest, or checksum) is a fixed-length string of
        characters generated by running data through a mathematical function called a <strong>hash function</strong>. No
        matter how large or small the input is, the output always has the same length.</p>

      <div class="analogy-box">
        <div class="label">Analogy</div>
        Think of a hash function like a <strong>meat grinder</strong>. You can feed in a tiny piece of meat or a whole
        cow — what comes out the other side is always the same shape and size (ground mince). Critically, you
        <em>cannot</em> reconstruct the original animal from the mince — it's a one-way process.
      </div>

      <p>Hash values are used across computing for data integrity verification, password storage, digital signatures,
        blockchain, and more. They are the invisible backbone of cybersecurity.</p>

