      <h3 class="sub-title">Key Properties of Hash Functions</h3>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🔒</div>
          <h4>Deterministic</h4>
          <p>Same input always produces the same output. "Hello" always hashes to the same digest.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">↔️</div>
          <h4>One-Way</h4>
          <p>You cannot reverse a hash to get the original input. It's a mathematical one-way street.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🌪️</div>
          <h4>Avalanche Effect</h4>
          <p>Changing even one character in the input completely changes the output hash.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">⚡</div>
          <h4>Fast to Compute</h4>
          <p>Hash functions are designed to be computationally efficient — millions per second.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🎯</div>
          <h4>Collision Resistant</h4>
          <p>Two different inputs should never produce the same output (though older algorithms fail here).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">📏</div>
          <h4>Fixed Length</h4>
          <p>Output is always the same length regardless of input size — a single byte or a terabyte.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
