    <div class="section" id="s2">
      <h2 class="section-title">2. How Hashing Works — Step by Step</h2>
      <p>Understanding the internal mechanism of hashing helps demystify why it's so powerful. Here's a simplified
        walkthrough of what happens when you hash a password or a file:</p>

      <ul class="step-list">
        <li>
          <p><strong>Input is received:</strong> You provide a string (e.g., your password <code class="inline">MyP@ssw0rd!</code>) or a file's contents to the hash function.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Padding is applied:</strong> The algorithm pads the input to a specific block size (e.g., SHA-256
            works on 512-bit blocks). Extra bits are added in a defined, reversible manner.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Block processing:</strong> The message is split into chunks. Each chunk is processed through a
            series of mathematical operations — bitwise XOR, AND, shifts, rotations, and modular additions.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Compression:</strong> Internal state variables (called registers or chaining values) are updated
            with each block. These become increasingly scrambled with each round.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><strong>Final digest:</strong> After all blocks are processed, the final state variables are concatenated
            to form the hash digest — a fixed-length hexadecimal string.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>The Avalanche Effect in Action:</strong><br>
        "password" → SHA-256 → <code>5e884898da28047151d0e56f8dc629...</code><br>
        "Password" → SHA-256 → <code>e7cf3ef4f17c3999a94f2c6f612e8a...</code><br><br>
        Just capitalizing one letter produces a completely different hash. This is by design.
      </div>

