          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>BLAKE2</td>
            <td>Variable</td>
            <td>Very Fast</td>
            <td>Strong</td>
            <td>✅ Modern</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="sub-title">MD5 — The Broken Classic</h3>
      <p>MD5 (Message Digest 5) was designed in 1991 by Ron Rivest. For years it was ubiquitous — used in SSL
        certificates, file integrity checks, and password storage. However, by 2004 researchers demonstrated full
        collision attacks, and by 2008 MD5-signed certificates were forged. It is <strong>completely broken for security
          purposes</strong> today.</p>

      <div class="code-block">
        <span class="comment"># MD5 example (Python)</span>
        <span class="key">import</span> hashlib
        result = hashlib.md5(<span class="val">b"hello"</span>).hexdigest()
        <span class="comment"># Output: 5d41402abc4b2a76b9719d911017c592</span>
        <span class="comment"># NEVER use MD5 for passwords or security!</span>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-title">SHA Family — NIST Standards</h3>
      <p>The <strong>Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA)</strong> family was developed by the NSA and standardized by NIST.
        SHA-256 (part of SHA-2) is the current workhorse of the internet — used in Bitcoin, TLS certificates, Git, and
        WordPress core integrity checks.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">bcrypt — Password Hashing Champion</h3>
      <p>bcrypt was specifically designed for password storage. Its key innovation: it is <strong>intentionally
          slow</strong>. By including a "cost factor" (work factor), bcrypt can be tuned to take 100ms or more to
        compute. This makes brute-force and dictionary attacks impractical even with modern hardware.</p>

