    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
    <div class="section" id="s4">
      <h2 class="section-title">4. Hash Collisions &amp; Attacks</h2>
      <p>A <strong>collision</strong> occurs when two different inputs produce the same hash output. While theoretically
        impossible in a perfect hash function, mathematical weaknesses can allow attackers to engineer this.</p>

      <h3 class="sub-title">Types of Hash Attacks</h3>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">📖</div>
          <h4>Dictionary Attack</h4>
          <p>Attacker precomputes hashes of common passwords and compares them against stolen hash databases.
            "password123" → hash → match found.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">💥</div>
          <h4>Brute Force</h4>
          <p>Every possible combination is tried. Fast for MD5/SHA-1 on modern GPUs (billions of hashes/sec), but
            bcrypt/Argon2 make this infeasible.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">🌈</div>
          <h4>Rainbow Table</h4>
          <p>Pre-computed tables mapping plaintext → hash for millions of passwords. Defeated by
            <strong>salting</strong> — adding random data before hashing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">💀</div>
          <h4>Collision Attack</h4>
          <p>Two different inputs crafted to produce the same hash. Used to forge digital certificates. MD5 and SHA-1
            are vulnerable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

