  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <div class="section" id="s6">
    <h2 class="section-title">6. WordPress Password Hashing — Deep Dive</h2>
    <p>When you create a WordPress account and set a password, WordPress doesn't store your password. It stores a
      <strong>hash</strong> of it. When you log in, WordPress hashes your input and compares it to the stored hash.
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">The phpass Library</h3>
    <p>WordPress uses a custom implementation based on <strong>phpass</strong> (pronounced "pH pass") — a portable
      password hashing framework. The default algorithm is a <strong>Portable Hash</strong> scheme using MD5 with 8
      rounds of stretching — specifically designed to be backward-compatible.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Important:</strong> As of WordPress 6.x, passwords for new user accounts use <code>bcrypt</code> on
      systems that support it (PHP 5.5+), while the phpass portable hash remains as a fallback. On update, old hashes
      are transparently upgraded on next successful login.
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-title">How WordPress Stores a Password</h3>
    <ul class="step-list">
      <li>
        <p><strong>User submits password</strong> "MySecret123" via the registration or update form.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>WordPress calls</strong> <code class="inline">wp_hash_password()</code> — which internally calls
          <code class="inline">$wp_hasher-&gt;HashPassword()</code>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Random salt generated:</strong> A 22-character random salt is created using <code class="inline">wp_generate_password()</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Iterated hashing:</strong> The password + salt are hashed through MD5 iteratively (2^8 = 256
          rounds). Each round feeds output back as input.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Result stored</strong> in <code class="inline">wp_users</code> table as a string like <code class="inline">$P$BucDVHR...</code></p>
      </li>
    </ul>

