            <polygon points="0 0, 7 3.5, 0 7" fill="#2e7d52"></polygon>
          </marker>
        </defs>
      </svg>
      <p class="diagram-caption">Fig 6: WordPress password hashing during storage and login verification</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-title">The WordPress Password Hash Format</h3>
    <p>A stored WordPress password looks like: <code class="inline">$P$BucDVHR7wPLLGpEhZdGrOt5Ic0KWLJ1</code></p>

    <table class="hash-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Segment</th>
          <th>Value</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>$P$</td>
          <td>$P$</td>
          <td>Algorithm identifier (phpass portable)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cost factor</td>
          <td>B</td>
          <td>2^8 = 256 hash rounds</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Salt</td>
          <td>ucDVHR7wPLLG</td>
          <td>22 random characters (128-bit salt)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hash</td>
          <td>pEhZdGrOt5Ic0KWLJ1</td>
          <td>Base64-encoded iterated MD5 hash</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <hr class="section-div">

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <div class="section" id="s7">
    <h2 class="section-title">7. File Integrity &amp; Checksums in WordPress</h2>
    <p>Beyond passwords, WordPress uses hash values to verify that its core files haven't been tampered with. This is
      critical for detecting malware injections and supply-chain attacks.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">WordPress Core File Verification</h3>
    <p>Every official WordPress release comes with a set of SHA-256 checksums published by WordPress.org. Security
      plugins like Wordfence and Sucuri compare local file hashes against these known-good values to detect
      intrusions.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
      <span class="comment"># Verify a WordPress file via WP-CLI</span>
      $ wp core verify-checksums
      <span class="val">Success: WordPress installation verifies against checksums.</span>

      <span class="comment"># Or manually compute a file hash</span>
      $ sha256sum wp-login.php
      <span class="val">3a7f29d8b7c4c2e1... wp-login.php</span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-title">How Malware Detection Works</h3>
    <ul class="step-list">
      <li>
        <p><strong>Download fresh checksums</strong> from the WordPress.org API for your version.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Compute SHA-256</strong> hash of every local WordPress core file.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Compare</strong> local hashes against the official ones.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Flag differences:</strong> If a hash doesn't match, the file has been modified — possibly injected
          with malicious code.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Alert and remediate:</strong> Replace compromised files from a clean download.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="analogy-box">
      <div class="label">Real-World Analogy</div>
      Imagine a sealed box of medicine. The pharmacist puts a <strong>tamper-evident seal</strong> with a unique ID.
      When you receive it, you look up that ID on the manufacturer's database. If the seal is broken or the ID doesn't
      match — the medicine was tampered with. WordPress checksums work the same way.
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr class="section-div">

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <div class="section" id="s8">
    <h2 class="section-title">8. WordPress Security Threats Involving Hash Values</h2>

    <h3 class="sub-title">1. Database Breach &amp; Password Hash Dumping</h3>
    <p>SQL injection attacks can expose the entire <code class="inline">wp_users</code> table, including hashed
      passwords. Attackers then try to crack these offline using tools like Hashcat or John the Ripper.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">2. wp-config.php Exposure</h3>
    <p>If <code class="inline">wp-config.php</code> is exposed (via misconfigured servers, directory traversal),
      attackers gain the secret keys used for HMAC — allowing them to forge authentication cookies without knowing any
      password.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">3. Plugin/Theme Supply Chain Attacks</h3>
    <p>Malicious actors have uploaded modified versions of popular plugins to unofficial repositories. Checksum
      verification helps detect these — if a plugin's file hashes don't match the official release, it's been tampered
      with.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">4. Cookie Forgery via HMAC Weakness</h3>
    <p>WordPress authentication cookies are signed with HMAC using the secret keys. If the hashing algorithm is weak
      or keys are short, attackers can forge cookies to impersonate any user including administrators.</p>

    <div class="pros-cons">
      <div class="pros-box">
        <h4>✅ Protections That Hashing Provides</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Passwords are never stored in plaintext</li>
          <li>Rainbow table attacks prevented via salting</li>
          <li>File integrity verified post-deployment</li>
          <li>Nonces prevent CSRF attacks</li>
          <li>HMAC-signed cookies prevent session hijacking</li>
          <li>Plugin authenticity can be verified</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cons-box">
        <h4>⚠️ Limitations &amp; Risks</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Weak passwords are still crackable even when hashed</li>
          <li>Old phpass MD5-based hashes are vulnerable if exposed</li>
          <li>Compromised secret keys invalidate all session security</li>
          <li>File integrity only works for core — not plugins/themes</li>
          <li>Salting can be bypassed with offline GPU attacks on weak passwords</li>
          <li>No protection against zero-day exploits in plugins</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr class="section-div">

  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <div class="section" id="s9">
    <h2 class="section-title">9. Salting &amp; Key Stretching</h2>

    <h3 class="sub-title">What is a Salt?</h3>
    <p>A <strong>salt</strong> is a random string of data added to a password <em>before</em> hashing. Each user gets
      a unique, randomly generated salt stored alongside their hash. This ensures that even two users with identical
      passwords have completely different hashes.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
      <span class="comment"># Without salt (VULNERABLE)</span>
      hash(<span class="val">"password123"</span>) = <span class="val">482c811da5d5b4bc6d497ffa98491e38</span>
      <span class="comment"># Two users with same password → SAME hash → one crack breaks both</span>

      <span class="comment"># With salt (PROTECTED)</span>
      salt1 = <span class="val">"aX9bZ2"</span>
      hash(<span class="val">"password123"</span> + salt1) = <span class="val">9f2b3d4c8a1e6f7...</span>

      salt2 = <span class="val">"mK5pQ8"</span>
      hash(<span class="val">"password123"</span> + salt2) = <span class="val">3e7a1b9c4f2d8e5...</span>
      <span class="comment"># Same password → DIFFERENT hashes → cracking one reveals nothing about the other</span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Key Stretching (Iterated Hashing)</h3>
    <p><strong>Key stretching</strong> deliberately makes hashing slow by repeating the hash operation thousands or
      millions of times. This increases the time needed to brute-force a password from milliseconds to seconds —
      multiplying the attacker's work by orders of magnitude.</p>

