  <!-- SECTION 10 -->
  <div class="section" id="s10">
    <h2 class="section-title">10. Real-World Case Studies</h2>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Case Study 1: The 2019 Wordfence WordPress Hash Cracking Campaign</h3>
    <p>In 2019, security researchers at Wordfence tracked a large-scale credential-stuffing campaign against WordPress
      sites. Attackers used previously breached databases (from sites that stored MD5-hashed passwords without salts)
      to attempt logins on WordPress installs. Sites using older password hashing schemes or weak passwords were
      compromised.</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Proper salted hashing + strong passwords blocks credential stuffing. Enable 2FA as a
      second layer.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Case Study 2: TimThumb Vulnerability (2011)</h3>
    <p>The TimThumb image resizing script, used by thousands of WordPress themes, allowed remote file inclusion.
      Attackers injected PHP shells. File integrity checking (comparing hashes) would have detected the modified files
      immediately. This breach affected hundreds of thousands of sites before being identified.</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Regular checksum verification of all theme and plugin files can catch injections
      before they cause damage.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Case Study 3: Panama Papers Leak (WordPress Connection)</h3>
    <p>The law firm Mossack Fonseca, whose data was leaked in the Panama Papers, ran a WordPress portal. Investigators
      found the portal was running an outdated version with unpatched plugins. Attackers gained access through a
      plugin vulnerability, potentially exfiltrated authentication hashes, and pivoted to internal systems.</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Keeping WordPress and its ecosystem updated is critical — hash-based authentication is
      only as secure as the underlying system integrity.</p>

    <h3 class="sub-title">Case Study 4: Password Hash Exposure via phpMyAdmin</h3>
    <p>A common misconfiguration leaves phpMyAdmin accessible without authentication. Attackers who find these can
      directly download the <code class="inline">wp_users</code> table, extract password hashes, and run them through
      Hashcat. A 6-character MD5 password can be cracked in under a second on modern hardware.</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Secure database admin interfaces, enforce strong passwords, and use bcrypt/Argon2 for
      hashing.</p>
  </div>

  <hr class="section-div">

  <!-- SECTION 11 -->
  <div class="section" id="s11">
    <h2 class="section-title">11. Best Practices for Hash Security in WordPress</h2>

    <div class="cards-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">🔑</div>
        <h4>Regenerate Secret Keys</h4>
        <p>After any suspected breach, regenerate your WordPress secret keys immediately. This invalidates all active
          sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">🔐</div>
        <h4>Force Strong Passwords</h4>
        <p>Use plugins like "Password Policy Manager" to enforce minimum complexity. bcrypt of "hunter2" is still
          weak.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">🛡️</div>
        <h4>Two-Factor Authentication</h4>
        <p>Even if a password hash is cracked, 2FA prevents unauthorized access. Google Authenticator plugin works
          well.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">🔍</div>
        <h4>File Integrity Monitoring</h4>
        <p>Use Wordfence or Sucuri to run regular SHA-256 checksums of all files. Set up email alerts for changes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">📦</div>
        <h4>Keep Everything Updated</h4>
        <p>WordPress core, themes, and plugins should be auto-updated. Outdated code is the #1 attack vector.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon">🚫</div>
        <h4>Protect wp-config.php</h4>
        <p>Move wp-config.php above the web root, restrict file permissions to 400, and block direct access via
          .htaccess.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="code-block">
      <span class="comment"># Protect wp-config.php via .htaccess</span>
      &lt;files wp-config.php&gt;
      <span class="key">order</span> allow,deny
      <span class="key">deny</span> from all
      &lt;/files&gt;

      <span class="comment"># Disable file editing in WordPress admin</span>
      <span class="key">define</span>( <span class="val">'DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT'</span>, true );

      <span class="comment"># Force SSL</span>
      <span class="key">define</span>( <span class="val">'FORCE_SSL_ADMIN'</span>, true );
    </div>
  </div>

